
= Background
This section presents relevant research and frameworks to establish an overview of research on therapist interfaces for rehabilitation through exergames. The chapter covers the following areas: (1) Exergames & Automation in Rehabilitation, (2) More-than-Human Agency, and (3) Therapist Interfaces for Exergame-Based Therapy.


== Exergames & Automation in Rehabilitation

Exergames, a fusion of "exercise" and "video games", are video games that promote physical activity and cognitive engagement through gamified tasks (Oh & Yang, 2010). Although not exclusively, they are often classified under a broader category "Serious Games", which refers to digital games designed primarily for purposes beyond entertainments, such as education, training, and health interventions (Maggio, 2025). The researches also differentiate between commercial off-the-shelf systems and custom systems used for rehabilitation purposes (Allegue et al.,2022). The meta-analyses show exergames systems specifically designed for rehabilitation tend to be more effective than conventional therapy, whereas re-purposed commercial off-the-shelf systems often yield mixed or neutral results, in part because their interaction mechanics and feedback are not tightly aligned with therapeutic goals (Maier et al. 2019). Exergames have shown to support neuroplasticity by enabling high repetition and high intensity task-oriented activities that are challenging enough but not frustrating resulting in increased motivation and engagement (Maier et al., 2019; Allegue et al.,2022; @shah2022). 

A wide range of technologies have been explored to implement exergames for rehabilitation. Non-immersive systems based on Xbox Kinect, Nintendo Wii, PlayStation, Robot-assisted serious games, or similar motion-sensing consoles are widely studied and have shown be be clinically provide effective rehabilitation compared to conventional therapy (Cheok et al., 2015; Santos et al., 2025, Doumas et al., 2025). Similarly, XR-based exergames, which are more immersive in nature, have been used for motor and cognitive rehabilitation after stroke and other neurological conditions, with studies reporting improvements in motor functions, spatial attention, and improved engagement compared to conventional exercises (@fordell2016 @faria2016 ). These studies demonstrate that exergames can be implemented and are being explored in varied technologies across different rehabilitation settings.


