// Exergame-based rehabilitation systems, alone, can also be seen as more-than-human configurations in which therapists, patients, *XR* exergames, sensing devices and AI-based recommendation or adaptation mechanism jointly perform therapy. AI-enabled exergames and adaptive systems adjust task difficulty, feedback, and progression based on continuous performance data, moving part of clinical decision-making into algorithmic logic (source?) 
 
Computational systems are no longer just tools "used by" humans, rather they are co-creators of actions, phenomena and experiences (Frauenberger, 2019). In relation to these systems, humans are not acting alone. The fourth wave emphasizes the need to move away from the notion of human-centered design where humans are *autonomous* decision-makers interacting with neutral tools, or view computational systems as autonomous tools that simply replace humans. Instead*, the 4th wave* view them as co-performers where humans and computational systems continuously shape each other and perform social practices together (Frauenberger, 2019; Kuijer & Giaccardi, 2018). As such, agency is not something human posses individually or independently, but rather relational and distributed across relationships between humans, technologies, institutions, environments, and norms. 

Therefore, from a more-than-human perspective, agency in rehabilitation is distributed across clinicians, therapists, patients, exergame systems, algorithms, interfaces, institutions, and even regulatory frameworks, rather than being possessed by any single actor (Frauenberger, 2019). Even in clinical context, it is emphasized that clinicians should still be able to intervene when co-performing with AI, because legal and ethical responsibility for decisions currently remains with them (Olawade et al., 2026). The human agency is thus not replaced by AI because it is central to the process and continuously negotiated with the system (Frauenberger, 2019). Although, delegating more decision-making to the autonomous systems can create power imbalance. For this reason, a more balanced approach where systems "performs" but the judgement of what is good, bad or appropriate ultimately remains with the humans is recommended (Kuijer & Giaccardi, 2018).

The sense of agency can be affected by factors such as timing, predictability, and level or form of automation (Legaspi et al, 2024; Limerick, 2014). Experimental studies indicate that higher level of automation and increased mental workload are associated with reduced sense of agency, whereas thoughtfully designed hybrid interactions can maintain a stronger feeling of control even when systems act on user's behalf (Howard et al., 2016; Legaspi et al., 2024). These findings are particularly relevant in clinical contexts where therapists must be able to stand by decisions, both ethically and legally, even when those clinical decisions are mediated by automated systems (Olawade?).

*In a world where it is no longer exclusively humans that act, design, make use, and change, more-than-human design offers a theoretical lens for understanding rehabilitation exergames as an integrated collection of humans (therapists and patients) and non-humans (such as exergame software, algorithms, data representations, sensors, infrastructure), the agency of which are continuously negotiated (Giacardi & Redström, 2020).* As these system become more data-driven, healthcare decisions are increasingly influenced by algorithmic logic and historical data apart from just therapist reasoning (source?). This means, in the context of exergame-based rehabilitation, therapy outcomes would be shaped not only by therapists' decisions and patients' performance, but also by how algorithms classify performance, how data is presented, what data is shown or hidden, what technology is used, and even institutional and legal constraints. This reiterates the fact that design in therapeutic scenarios is more about configuring relationships and agencies among multiple human and nonhuman actors over time, rather than just optimizing features within an interface. 



